course: money
lesson: 32
title: The school fair, famous companies and being your own boss
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A school fair is a small business day: every stall has costs, takings and, if it is planned well, a profit that goes to a good cause.
explain: Picture Year 5's cake stall: parents baked the cakes for free, so 40 cakes at 50p bring in £20 and all of it is profit for the school.
explain: Giant companies started small too: Apple, the famous story goes, began with two young men in a garage and now sells phones to the whole world; big companies are often owned by thousands of people who each hold small pieces called shares.
explain: Being your own boss means freedom, but also risk: no boss shouting, but no sick pay, long hours, and often a loss in the first year before any profit comes.
explain: Every stall and shop needs the same check: will the money in be more than the money out?
fact: Fundraising is collecting money for a good cause; when things are donated, all the money taken is profit for the cause, like 40 × 50p = £20.
fact: A guessing game at 20p a go with 60 players takes 60 × 20p = £12; if the jar of sweets cost £3, it raises £12 − £3 = £9.
fact: Unit cost is the cost of making one item: thread at £2 for 10 bracelets is £2 ÷ 10 = 20p each.
fact: Margin is the profit on each item: a bracelet sold for £1 that cost 20p makes 80p, so 10 sold make 10 × 80p = £8.
fact: A company is a business, and its founder is the person who started it; many famous companies, such as Apple, started small, and Apple was reported to have over 100,000 workers.
fact: A share is a small piece of a company; owning 1 share out of 100 means owning 1 hundredth of it, and a shareholder gets a part of the profits.
fact: Self-employed people work for themselves; a week off ill can mean £0 that week, while an employed worker often gets sick pay.
fact: A start-up is a new business; many lose money at first, like costs of £20,000 and takings of £15,000, a loss of £5,000.
fact: To break even means money in equals money out, so there is no profit and no loss; a business with costs of £20,000 that takes £20,000 breaks even, and if takings grow to £28,000 it makes £8,000 profit.
fact: A tuck shop with costs of £10 and takings of £18 makes £8 profit, which is £2 each for 4 children, or £8 for charity.
fact: Small businesses often need very long hours: a corner shop open 7 days for 12 hours a day is open 84 hours a week.
fact: If prizes cost more than the money taken, a stall is loss-making: 20 players at 50p bring £10, and prizes of £15 mean a loss of £5.
exercise: Year 5 sells 40 donated cakes at 50p. How much do they take? | £20 | £40 | £2 | £90
exercise: Why is all £20 from the cake stall profit? | The cakes were donated, so there were no costs | Cake stalls never have to pay for anything | The school paid for the cakes afterwards | The teacher kept the costs a secret
exercise: 60 people guess the sweets at 20p a guess. How much comes in? | £12 | £120 | £80 | £6
exercise: The jar of sweets cost £3 and the game took £12. How much was raised? | £9 | £15 | £12 | £3
exercise: Thread for 10 bracelets costs £2. What does one bracelet cost to make? | 20p | £2 | 10p | £20
exercise: Josh sells each bracelet for £1. It cost 20p. What is the profit on one? | 80p | £1.20 | 20p | £1
exercise: Josh sells all 10 bracelets. What is his total profit? | £8 | £10 | £2 | £12
exercise: How did Apple start, according to the famous story? | Two young men in a garage | A king who gave them a palace | A government that built it from scratch | A giant company that split in two
exercise: Who owns a big company like McDonald's? | Lots of people who own small bits called shares | Only the person who serves the burgers | Nobody, because big companies own themselves | The government of every country it is in
exercise: A company has 100 shares and Kai owns 1. How much of it does he own? | 1 out of 100 | Half of it | All of it | 100 out of 1
exercise: Ellie's uncle runs his own business and is ill for a week. What does he likely earn that week? | £0, because there is no sick pay | Double, because he is the boss | The same as always, from the council | £100, from his customers' kindness
exercise: Leo's mum's salon costs £20,000 and takes £15,000 in its first year. What happened? | A loss of £5,000 | A profit of £5,000 | A profit of £35,000 | She broke even
exercise: In year two, costs are £20,000 and takings £28,000. What is the profit? | £8,000 | £48,000 | £28,000 | £2,000
exercise: The tuck shop costs £10 and takes £18. What is the profit? | £8 | £28 | £18 | £10
exercise: The corner shop is open 7 days a week, 12 hours a day. How many hours a week? | 84 | 19 | 72 | 74
exercise: Ben's stall takes £10 but his prizes cost £15. What happened? | He lost £5 | He made £5 | He made £25 | He broke even
test: Your mate goes, "Being your own boss is easy money, no cap, you just chill." ("No cap" means honestly.) What's true? | It brings freedom but also risk, long hours and no sick pay | He's right, bosses never work and always earn more | Own-boss people get paid even when they don't work | It's illegal to be your own boss under 50
test: The teacher says to Ben, "You're losing money, pal!" His prizes cost £15 and 20 kids played at 50p. What would fix it? | Cheaper prizes or a higher price per go | Giving away even bigger prizes | Letting everyone play for free | Charging less per go and adding prizes
test: An Aussie uncle at the fair says, "I'll have a crack at the sweets jar, it's only 20c, heaps of fun." ("Have a crack" means try; "20c" is twenty cents.) If 60 people play at 20 cents, what is taken? | 12 dollars | 120 dollars | 6 dollars | 80 dollars
test: Josh says, "My bracelets cost me £2 and I sold 10 for a quid each, so I made a tenner." ("Quid" means a pound.) What's the real profit? | £8 | £10 | £12 | £2
test: Leo's mate says, "His mum's salon lost money, so she's rubbish at business." What's the fairer view? | Many new businesses lose money at first; hers made a profit in year two | He's right, a first-year loss means the business has failed | Salons can never make a profit | She should have charged nothing to get customers
test: What would happen if the cake stall had to buy the 40 cakes at 30p each? | Profit would fall from £20 to £8 | Profit would rise from £20 to £32 | Profit would stay at £20 | They would lose £12
test: In a cartoon, a kid buys "one share of McDonald's" and gets the keys to every restaurant. What's wrong? | One share is a tiny piece of the company, not the whole thing | Nothing, one share gives you every restaurant | Shares are only sold to people who work there | Shares are tickets for free burgers for life
test: Grace asks, "Why does the corner shop man work seven days, is he mad?" Her mum, from Cork, says, "Ah, small shops don't make a fortune, girl, he works to keep it going." What's true? | Small shops often need very long hours to earn enough | Corner shop owners are paid by the government | He works long hours because he's bored at home | Small shops make more money than supermarkets
test: The tuck shop made £8 profit. The class votes: split between the 4 kids who ran it, or — no wait, between 8 kids. How much each? | £1 | £2 | £8 | £4
test: Ruby goes, "Apple was always huge, innit." What's the real story? | It started small, the story goes in a garage, and grew by making things people wanted | It began as a huge company with thousands of workers | The government built it in one day | It was handed to its founders by a king
test: Ellie's uncle says, "No boss shouting at me, sound as." ("Sound as" means really good.) What does he give up for that? | Sick pay and a steady wage | Weekends with no customers ever | The chance to choose his own hours | Every penny of his profit
exam: It is given that forty cakes, donated without charge, are sold at fifty pence each. Determine the profit realised. | £20 | £40 | £2 | £90
exam: Ascertain the unit cost of a bracelet where materials sufficient for ten bracelets are procured for the sum of £2. | 20p | £2 | 10p | £20
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a share in a company? | A small portion of the ownership of the company, entitling the holder to part of its profits | A ticket conferring the right to purchase goods from the company without charge | The entire ownership of the company and all of its premises | A loan advanced by the company to each of its employees
exam: Identify the principal disadvantage of self-employment as described in this lesson. | The absence of pay during periods of illness and the bearing of all risk | The presence of a manager directing every action | The prohibition of working on more than three days a week | The obligation to share all profit with the government
